ce optical color mixing is clearly  a play in my work while you may see pink  here the painting is made up only of  three factual colors red white and dark  green but because the lines are very  fine  the colors mix optically and the white  and red create the illusion of pink on  the surface here's a detail shot a  close-up view of the painting there's  another one that has quite a bit of  optical mixing and viewing the painting  from a great distance the optical mixing  is more convincing as we step closer to  the painting detail shot the factual  colors begin to be revealed ironically I  never took a color class while I was an  undergraduate at Yale though I did often  walk through what was called the Albers  corridor of prints at the Yale art  gallery my own paintings deal very much  with color and I only came to study the  Albers volume after years of painting  when I began to teach in that sense I  learned about color very much how Albers  would want one to learn about color by  seeing color and by using it when I  first started looking at the Albers  folio I was amazed at how clearly it  explicate it and augmented what I  already knew to be true about color but  even though I had worked with color for  many years and had experienced colors  instability firsthand on a regular basis  I still found myself in a slight state  of disbelief at times I even assured  myself that one of the plates was a miss  prynt because I could not believe what I  saw when I lifted the flap perhaps my  temporary experience of disbelief is key  to understanding the importance of the  interaction of color  we know that the reversal of theory and  practice or the understanding of colors  relatively were not in it's in  themselves groundbreaking so what makes  Albers different for one thing Albers  interaction of color was born out of  Albers own teaching its nature is  collaborative and greater than one  person singular vision Albers provided  the questions and the structures though  many today associate the work only with  Albers Albers himself was clear in the  volume that his students were his  collaborators the majority of the plates  are the best example of his students  responses to his questions and exercises  the book itself is the result of  experience of countless hours of  numerous people seeing color but there  is something else here that is  groundbreaking and while the use of  colored paper seems simple and obvious  to us now it provides a key to Albers  success Albers writes color paper  permits a repeated use of precisely the  same color without the slightest change  in tone light or surface quality we can  choose from a large collection of tones  displayed in front of us and can thus  constantly compare neighboring and  contrasting colors this offers a  training which no palette can provide  this method mirrors the concept of the  scientific control and unchanging or  fixed group of colors can be compared to  their variable counterparts the student  had the ability to compare back and  forth between factual and actual color  via colored paper we who view the folio  have the ability to compare factual and  actual via the masks flaps and folds in  the original folio the new iPad app  includes this function and augments it  with movement we can move pieces of  color around on the studies just as the  student could before gluing down the  collage the ability to create these  comparisons is not something one sees in  a painting while I may have the  experience of moving color from pallet  to canvas this remains usually a private  experience the ability for comparison  continues to be central to the  wide-ranging success of the interaction  of color I remember meeting with a  student who said she didn't want to  learn about the rules of color because  that would demystify the experience of  color for her but to me color seemed so  inexhaustible that no matter how much I  study and contain it it surprises and  alludes albers allows us to hold on to  the factual in our minds as a kind of  zero degrees from which to leap even if  we may never really access factual color  it remains a necessary mental framework  and as much as Albers verbally verbally  rejected the rule based color systems  that preceded him he created his very  own effective rules and systems to teach  and deal with color I think it's the  discipline in the face of color that  makes him so successful as a teacher and  so widely accessed outside of the field  people can relate to the rules  although Albers rules make fun of rules  we can also consider Albers relationship  to the philosophers of his day  for example maurice merleau-ponty who  championed the field of philosophy known  as phenomenology asked the question  about where truth resides he used the  example of a tile pattern in a swimming  pool distorted by the light passing  through water he said that most people  see this as an illusion believing that  there is some fixed truth out there  the grid under the swimming pool that is  being distorted by our perception or by  the conditions around the pool like  Albers he notes that this belief is  something of a delusion and he proposes  that we reverse this hierarchy of what  we know over what we see can't we see  the distortions as a valid reality in  fact they were the only valid reality  for merleau-ponty he says when through  the waters thickness I see the tiled  bottom of the pool I do not see it  despite the water and the reflections I  see it through them and because of them  but there is some deeper thing about  Albers project that is always on the tip  of my tongue it's not that interaction  of color demonstrated merleau-ponty x'  ideas visually but that his work also  simultaneously tugs away at it like  staring at red for too long and our eye  compensating with the green while Albers  championed the actual he acknowledges  the factual rather than rejecting fact  or essence as irrelevant he affirms the  necessary role 