etween two colors and  create an illusion of transparency even  when all the colour is present are  opaque we can use that illusion to  create spatial illusions in colour in  this case the more dominant the yellow  is over the red the more it advances in  space  album Albers also addresses optical  color mixture which is a different type  of color illusion than an after image  when the bits of color are small enough  and that's close enough together our I  will optically mix the two or more  colors in this instance two brick walls  are made of the same red but the black  mortar makes the overall picture seem  darker while the white mortar makes the  overall picture seem lighter the bezel  defect is a certain type of optical  mixture named after ville hi Yvonne  build a run rug designer who noticed  that when he changed one color thread in  his rug designs the entire design would  change but optical mixture has been used  for centuries in mosaics tapestries more  recently an impressionist painting and  pointillism in Albers time as the photo  mechanical printing process such as  halftone reproductions in newspapers  where greyscale is created by using only  varying proportions of black dots on a  white ground and in our time optical  mixture exists on every digital print  and computer screen or projection we  look at including this one the pixels  the ultimate use of optical mixture with  bits so tiny we cannot even recognize  them as such  so the images I've shown thus far are  the more pared down and instructional  images in the Albers folio and Albers  admits that quote these studies permit  hardly any self expression end quote  but there are many other studies in the  folio that are quite complicated and  dynamic many of these can be found in  the free study section so here's some  examples of the free studies in addition  to performing the systematic systematic  exercises Albers students also made  independent works the systematic studies  occupied most of the in-class time while  the free studies were done his homework  throughout the Albers course  there are numerous beautiful plates in  this volume and the I paddle app app  allows you to see all of them in the  most immediate way in a more immediate  way than I've ever experienced the app  also allows you not only to view the  plates but to rework the studies  themselves and anybody can do this no  matter what your background in art or  color this is how you can learn to see  color by trial and error and by doing  the exercises so if you go to you can go  to my studies and all of the plates  appear as blanks so this was a study  that I did based off of the format of  one of the outburst uh DS but I added my  own colors so you can basically choose a  palette and pull these things out and  change just with the touch of a finger  of the colors so it's like working with  cut paper all of the Albers exercises  were done with cut paper because Albers  didn't want his students to get bogged  down with mixing pigments and being  distracted by that he wanted the process  to be about seeing so the app in some  way even augments that further by  allowing you access to an Eva even wider  range of of colors at your fingertips  okay so now that you've seen some of the  plates from the Albers volume I want to  kind of continue my presentation with a  broader question which is what makes  Albers so continually influential and  still relevant to our present moment so  we might turn to other thinkers and  artists who are kindred spirits of  Albers to answer this question or to  consider it I speak less about direct  influence and more about shared  perceptual and cultural experiences  that yields to more interest methods and  conclusions  so Albers idea of putting practice  before theory was not in itself  groundbreaking in remarks on color  Ludwig Wittgenstein says quote to  describe the game of tennis is not to  teach it to him and vice-versa  here Vidkun stein reflects on  philosopher and mathematician Bertrand  Russell's two types of knowledge  knowledge by description and knowledge  by acquaintance in relation to color  knowledge by description is being able  to describe the rules of language for  instance or tennis or mathematics while  knowledge by acquaintance is being able  to perform the rules of those things via  experience and this is how we actually  learn language we don't learn language  by diagramming sentences we learn  language by singing language as an  infant in a very experience away and so  Albers teaches color by acquaintance and  certainly painters and scientists knew  about simultaneous contrast after images  an optical mixture for many years before  Albers for example Edgar Degas says  painting is the ability to surround a  Venetian red so it looks like a  Vermillion a Venetian red is a dull red  and vermilion is a brilliant saturated  red  so for daga painting is not about  putting down the bright saturated color  but about creating a set of context so  that the dull color may appear bright  it's something that painters have used  in their work as they move paint from  the palette to the context of the  painting the color inevitably changes  before them painters call this factual  color in isolation local color and have  long understood the limitations of  painting with only local color in mind  another aspect of color that Albers only  touches upon in the interaction of color  is that in addition to colors optical  instability color changes according to  lighting conditions there is lie  that is warm light that is cool harsh  light that emphasizes contrast low light  that diminishes it so this is something  that has occupied painters such as Monet  who painted the cathedral at Rouen  repeatedly during different times the  day as another way of showing us the  fluidity of color and perception so I'm  going to show you some of my recent  paintings so perhaps you'll see what  draws me to the work of Josef Albers for  instance optical color mixing is clearly  a play in my work while you may see pink  here the painting is made up only of  three factual colors red white and dark  green but because the lines are very  fine  the colors mix optically and the white  and red create the illusion of pink on  the surface here's a detail shot a  close-up view of the painting there's  another one that has quite a bit of  optical mixing and viewing the painting  from a great distance the optical mixing  is more convincing as we step closer to  the painting detail shot the factual  colors begin to be revealed ironically I  never took a color class while I was an  undergraduate at Yale though I did often  walk through what was called the Albers  corridor of prints at the Yale art  gallery my own paintings deal very much  with color and I only came to study the  Albers volume after years of painting  when I began to teach in that sense I  learned about color very much how Albers  would want one to learn about color by  seeing color and by using it when I  first started looking at the Albers  folio I was amazed at how clearly it  explicate it and augmented what I  already knew to be true about color but  even though I had worked with color for  many years and had experienced colors  instability firsthand on a regular basis  I still found myself in a slight state  of disbelief at times I even assured  myself that one of the plates was a miss  prynt because I could not believe what I  saw when I lifted the flap perhaps my  temporary experience of disbelief is key  to understanding the importance of the  interaction of color  we know that the reversal of theory and  practice or the understanding of colors  relatively were not in it's in  themselves groundbreaking so what makes  Albers different for one thing Albers  interaction of color was born out of  Albers own teaching its nature is  collaborative and greater than one  person singular vision Albers provided  the questions and the structures though  many today associate the work only with  Albers Albers himself was clear in the  volume that his students were his  collaborators the majority of the plates  are the best example of his students  responses to his questions and exercises  the book itself is the result of  experience of countless hours of  numerous people seeing color but there  is something else here that is  groundbreaking and while the use of  colored paper seems simple and obvious  to us now it provides a key to Albers  success Albers writes color paper  permits a repeated use of precisely the  same color without the slightest change  in tone light or surface quality we can  choose from a large collection of tones  displayed in front of us and can thus  constantly compare neighboring and  contrasting colors this offers a  training which no palette can provide  this method mirrors the concept of the  scientific control and unchanging or  fixed group of colors can be compared to  their variable counterparts the student  had the ability to compare back and  forth between factual and actual color  via colored paper we who view the folio  have the ability to compare factual and  actual via the masks flaps and folds in  the original folio the new iPad app  includes this function and augments it  with movement we can move pieces of  color around on the studies just as the  student could before gluing down the  collage the ability to create these  comparisons is not something one sees in  a painting while I may have the  experience of moving color from pallet  to canvas this remains usually a private  experience the ability for comparison  continues to be central to the  wide-ranging success of the interaction  of color I remember meeting with a  student who said she didn't want to  learn about the rules of color because  that would demystify the experience of  color for her but to me color seemed so  inexhaustible that no matter how much I  study and contain it it surprises and  alludes albers allows us to hold on to  the factual in our minds as a kind of  zero degrees from which to leap even if  we may never really access factual color  it remains a necessary mental framework  and as much as Albers verbally verbally  rejected the rule based color systems  that preceded him he created his very  own effective rules and systems to teach  and deal with color I think it's the  discipline in the face of color that  makes him so successful as a teacher and  so widely accessed outside of the field  people can relate to the rules  although Albers rules make fun of rules  we can also consider Albers relationship  to the philosophers of his day  for example maurice merleau-ponty who  championed the field of philosophy known  as phenomenology asked the question  about where truth resides he used the  example of a tile pattern in a swimming  pool distorted by the light passing  through water he said that most people  see this as an illusion believing that  there is some fixed truth out there  the grid under the swimming pool that is  being distorted by our perception or by  the conditions around the pool like  Albers he notes that this belief is  something of a delusion and he proposes  that we reverse this hierarchy of what  we know over what we see can't we see  the distortions as a valid reality in  fact they were the only valid reality  for merleau-ponty he says when through  the waters thickness I see the tiled  bottom of the pool I do not see it  despite the water and the reflections I  see it through them and because of them  but there is some deeper thing about  Albers project that is always on the tip  of my tongue it's not that interaction  of color demonstrated merleau-ponty x'  ideas visually but that his work also  simultaneously tugs away at it like  staring at red for too long and our eye  compensating with the green while Albers  championed the actual he acknowledges  the factual rather than rejecting fact  or essence as irrelevant he affirms the  necessary role it has to play in our  understanding of contingency ultimately  the terms factual and actual are  codependent we cannot understand the  actual without verifying the factual the  brilliance of Albers is that he used an  acknowledged fact to reverse the  established hierarchy a factual over  actual all of Albers structure serves to  celebrate the contingent and unruly  world of color for a long time  philosophers have debated whether truth  exists in essence or appearance but  Josef Albers volume enacts this quandary  more beautifully than anything I know  thank you  you
 

